    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail on the actual cellular-level signaling? Like, what is the mechanism where Leucine is \"sensed\" by the cell? I want to be able to explain to my client exactly how those intracellular signals translate to the actual building of new muscle tissue. Also, are there specific leucine thresholds (like grams per meal) that are most effective for hitting that mTOR trigger?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the cellular level, the leucine receptor (mTORC1 destabilizing factor) binds to leucine, which leads to the activation of Rag GTPase proteins that translocate mTORC1 to the lysosomal surface where it can be phosphorylated and activated by its upstream kinages such as AMPK and S6K1. Once activated, mTORC1 phosphorylates 4E-BP1 and S6K1, releasing eukaryotic translation initiation factor 4E (eIF4E) to promote cap-dependent translation of mRNAs encoding myofibrillar proteins like myosin and actin. Research generally suggests an anabolic threshold of approximately 2 to 3 grams of leucine per meal—roughly 2.5 to 3 grams of high-quality whey or egg protein—to achieve significant mTOR activation in resistance-trained adults. Exceeding this threshold provides diminishing returns on muscle protein synthesis rates but may offer marginal benefits for post-workout recovery or muscle endurance depending on the training intensity.",
      "source": "target",
